    "knowledge_point": "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus",
    "problem": "Let $f(t)=t\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{tan}⁡t}.$ Find ${g}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ given the function $g$ is defined by $g(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ for $0<x<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$",
    "worked_solution": "The function $f(t)=t\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{tan}⁡t}$ is continuous on $[0,x],$ where $0<x<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ So, FTC II applies, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{0}^{x}t\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{tan}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =x\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of ${g}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),$ we substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\sqrt[3]{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
